<h2 class="section-header">Scoring Methodology</h2>
<p style="font-size: 8pt; color: #4a5568; margin-bottom: 8pt;">
    Every company is scored on two independent axes. <strong>Business Quality</strong> asks whether this is a
    business worth owning; <strong>Valuation</strong> asks whether this is the price to own it at. Component
    scores are normalized against their measured S&amp;P&nbsp;500 distributions before weighting, and the
    rating is read from the pair of axes — a rich price alone can withhold a Buy but never produces a Sell.
</p>
<table class="data-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Axis</th><th>Component</th><th style="text-align: right;">Weight</th><th>Data Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td class="strong" rowspan="3">Business Quality</td><td>Capital Efficiency (ROIC vs WACC)</td><td class="num">35%</td><td style="font-size: 7.2pt;">NOPAT / invested capital vs CAPM cost of capital</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Financial Health</td><td class="num">35%</td><td style="font-size: 7.2pt;">Balance sheet, cash flows, profitability ratios</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Earnings Momentum</td><td class="num">30%</td><td style="font-size: 7.2pt;">Analyst EPS estimate revisions, beat/miss patterns</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="strong">Valuation</td><td>Price attractiveness</td><td class="num">&mdash;</td><td style="font-size: 7.2pt;">P/E, EV/EBITDA, PEG vs sector; blended intrinsic value</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="strong">Rating gate</td><td>Technical structure</td><td class="num">&mdash;</td><td style="font-size: 7.2pt;">A confirmed bearish structure steps a bullish rating down one tier</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p style="font-size: 7.2pt; color: #718096; margin-top: 4pt;">
    Rating matrix: high quality at an attractive price is a Strong Buy; high quality at a rich price is a Hold
    (watch, do not chase). Weak quality caps at Hold even when the price is attractive — cheap-but-weak is a
    value trap, not a short — and becomes a Sell at a fair price, a Strong Sell when the price offers no
    compensation. Sizing guidance is derived separately from risk level, conviction and the prevailing
    signal environment.
</p>

